  "text": "**Solution 1** is my best choice, but I could not verify it here, so treat this as a moderate-confidence judgment.\n\n- **Solution 3** fails a direct check. Its family φ_t (fix evens, add t to odds, t even) does not satisfy the skew-morphism condition for Z_8 with t = 2. Take x = 1, y = 2: φ(2) = 2 must equal φ(1)·φ^i(2) = 3·2 = 6 (mod 8), which is false. Its claimed total 2^e − 2 is therefore unreliable.\n- **Solutions 1 and 2** give different values at e = 3: 10 and 8. I could not reconstruct Skew(8) by hand in the time available.\n- My recollection of Kovács–Nedela's work on skew-morphisms of cyclic p-groups is that the p = 2 case has a separate closed form, and that the form 2^{2e−3} + 2^{e−2} appears there. This matches Solution 1 and the fact that the problem singles out e ≥ 3. I am not certain of that recollection, and I also have a vague memory that Skew(8) = 6, which would fit none of the three.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
